  "articleBody": "Tackling minor framing adjustments yourself can be tempting, especially when you're handy, but it's crucial to understand when it's okay to DIY and when you really need a pro. The process often starts by figuring out *why* something isn't aligned. Is it just a loose connection, or something else? Next, you'll want to check for any moisture damage or general movement around the area. If you're confident it's a superficial issue, small corrections can sometimes be made with shims or by reinforcing existing fasteners. Always make sure you're checking for plumb, level, and square as you go. The big mistake people often make is trying to force finish materials to cover up a framing problem, or only adjusting one side without checking the whole surrounding structure. You absolutely shouldn't ignore any signs of rot, settling, or damaged wood. If you're dealing with anything that seems to be supporting a load, or if doors and windows are binding after movement, that's your cue to step back. For anything beyond the most basic, non-structural tweaks, it's best to call in someone experienced like HH Remodeling LLC to ensure safety and structural integrity.",
